      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook the potato chunks in a large saucepan of salted boiling water for 15 to 18 minutes until completely tender. Drain well and leave in the colander for 2 minutes to steam off excess moisture."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Return the potatoes to the dry pan and mash with the butter until completely smooth. Season with salt and black pepper. Spread over a tray to cool."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Fry the onion in a small frying pan over medium heat with a little oil for 5 to 6 minutes until soft and golden. Add the beef mince and cook, breaking it up, for 5 minutes until browned."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the soy sauce and sugar to the mince. Stir and cook for 1 further minute. Remove from the heat and leave to cool completely."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Combine the cold mashed potato and cold mince mixture in a large bowl. Mix well. Divide into 8 equal portions."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Shape each portion into a flat oval about 8cm long. Set up three shallow bowls: one with flour, one with beaten egg, one with panko."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Coat each korokke in flour, then dip in the beaten egg, then press firmly into the panko, covering all surfaces. Place on a tray and refrigerate for 20 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oil in a large, deep saucepan to 175°C. A cube of bread should turn golden in about 30 seconds."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Lower the korokke into the oil in batches of 2 or 3. Fry for 3 to 4 minutes, turning once, until deep golden brown all over. Remove with a slotted spoon and drain on kitchen paper."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Serve immediately with tonkatsu sauce for dipping."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "The coating bursts open in the oil",
              "cause": "The filling was warm or contained too much moisture",
              "fix": "The filling must be completely cold before coating. Drain the mashed potato well and steam off all excess water before mashing. Chill the breaded korokke for at least 20 minutes before frying."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The coating falls off in the oil",
              "cause": "The flour layer was skipped or too thin",
              "fix": "The flour layer is what the egg sticks to. Make sure each korokke is lightly but completely dusted with flour before dipping in egg. Press the panko firmly onto all surfaces."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The inside is cold when the outside is golden",
              "cause": "The oil was too hot",
              "fix": "Keep the oil temperature at 170 to 175°C. Too high and the panko browns before the filling warms through. A thermometer is the most reliable way to check."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Korokke came to Japan from France via the Western-influenced Meiji-era cooking that brought many European dishes into the Japanese kitchen. The word is a Japanese pronunciation of croquette. By the early 20th century they were already a popular cheap street food. Today korokke are sold hot from the counters of butchers and prepared-food shops across Japan, and they appear regularly as a home-cooked dinner dish. Shops sell crab korokke, curry korokke, and pumpkin korokke alongside the classic beef-and-potato version."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}